23:45:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Ah, I see where that intuition comes from—and it’s a perfectly natural one! You’re thinking of a camera zoom lens, where more magnification makes distant things look bigger and closer. But with telescopes, there’s a hidden catch: **magnification alone is useless without resolution**. Think of a digital photo: you can zoom in on a face on your screen, but eventually you just see bigger and bigger pixels—no new detail appears. A telescope works the same way. The “pixels” are set by the **diffraction limit** of its mirror. No matter how much you crank up the magnification, you can’t squeeze out detail smaller than that limit. Here’s the key difference between a camera zoom and a telescope: - A camera lens zooming in on a nearby bird gathers more light from the bird itself, and the lens aperture (its diameter) is usually large enough relative to the distance to resolve those fine details. You’re limited by the lens’s resolution, but for many earthly scenes you never hit that limit because the bird is close and the lens is reasonably wide. - For the Moon, the flag is so far away that its angular size is incredibly tiny—far smaller than the “blur spot” (the Airy disk) that even a perfect telescope mirror produces. That blur spot is determined by the mirror’s diameter: the bigger the mirror, the smaller the blur. Hubble’s 2.4‑meter mirror creates a blur about 0.05 arcseconds wide. The flag’s entire width is just 0.0005 arcseconds—100 times smaller than that blur. So the flag is essentially a single “pixel” to Hubble; it’s smeared out and indistinguishable from the surrounding lunar soil. You might ask: “Then why not just build a mirror that’s 250 meters across to make that blur spot small enough?” In principle, yes—if you could build a perfect 250‑meter mirror in space, it *could* resolve the flag (about a few blur spots across). But physically, building such a mirror is far beyond current engineering. And even if you did, you’d run into another problem: the flag isn’t glowing on its own; it’s lit by sunlight, and the amount of light it reflects into that giant telescope would be so tiny that the image would be hopelessly lost in noise. It’s the same reason you can’t read a newspaper from across the country: even with a powerful telescope, the light gets spread out so much that you wouldn’t collect enough photons to see it. So the real answer is: yes, in theory, a big enough telescope *could* resolve the flag, but the required size is enormous (hundreds of meters) and the light-gathering problem would make it invisible anyway. It’s not that magnification has a magic limit—it’s that the laws of optics (diffraction) and photon counting make it practically impossible from Earth. The camera analogy breaks down because a camera lens is usually limited by the sensor’s pixel size, not by diffraction, for everyday distances. But when you push to astronomical distances, diffraction becomes the hard wall. So that “common sense” instinct is right on target—it just hits a wall called physics, not a lack of will to build a bigger telescope. 😊
