23:46:49 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s an optimistic and very human way to look at it — and you’re absolutely right that telescopes *have* gotten dramatically better, revealing things we once thought impossible. But the idea that “with enough progress we can see literally anything from any distance” bumps into a solid wall: not a technological one, but a **physical law barrier**. Some limits are written into the fabric of nature, not just our current engineering. Let’s unpack two of those hard limits: 1. **The diffraction limit** – resolution depends on mirror size. To resolve the flag (0.0005 arcseconds), you’d need a mirror about **250 metres across**. That’s physically *possible* to imagine one day, if we built it in space. So far, that’s an engineering problem — hard, but maybe not impossible in the distant future. 2. **The photon limit** – this is the real deal‑breaker. Even if you built that 250‑metre perfect space telescope, you’d be trying to see a beat‑up nylon flag illuminated by sunlight, lying on the glaringly bright Moon. The flag reflects only a tiny number of photons per second toward Earth. How tiny? A quick calculation: the flag gets hit by about 400 watts of sunlight per square metre, and reflects maybe 10% back into a hemisphere. Of that, the fraction that actually hits your telescope’s mirror is the mirror area divided by the area of a hemisphere at the Moon’s distance. For a 250‑metre mirror, that’s roughly one part in \(10^{13}\). So you’d collect roughly **a few hundred photons per second** from the whole flag — that sounds okay, but: - The Moon’s surface right next to the flag is *also* reflecting sunlight, creating a background *many billions of times brighter*. The flag would be completely lost in the glare, like a candle next to a nuclear explosion. - To separate the flag from the Moon’s surface, you’d need extraordinarily fine contrast — essentially, you’d need to detect the tiny colour difference (the flag is slightly more reflective in certain wavelengths). This would require an exposure so long that the Moon’s motion would smear it, and scattered light inside the telescope would swamp the signal. Even if you cooled the telescope to absolute zero and made it perfect, the randomness of photons (shot noise) would still bury the flag in statistical noise. You could integrate for months, but you still wouldn’t get a recognisable image — it’s physically impossible to beat the noise floor set by the Moon’s own brightness, because both the flag and the Moon are lit by the same Sun. It’s like trying to photograph a dim star right next to the Sun: no amount of telescope size can make the star visible against the Sun’s glare in visible light, because the background is just too bright. This is the **information limit**: the flag is simply not sending enough distinct “bits” of information (unique photons with spatial contrast) to Earth to reconstruct an image. You could build a telescope the size of the Earth, and you’d still be starved of the signal‑to‑noise ratio you need. The laws of thermodynamics and information theory don’t care about our technological progress — they’re non‑negotiable. Think of it this way: you can’t read a newspaper lying on a brightly lit mountain peak from across the country, even with any conceivable lens, because the brightness of the mountain itself would wash out the print. That’s not a lens problem; it’s that the newspaper and the mountain are both sending you light, and you can’t isolate the newspaper’s few stray photons from the mountain’s flood. So progress will let us see ever smaller and more distant things — we might one day resolve exoplanet surfaces, or see the atmospheres of planets around other stars. But “literally anything from any distance” would mean violating some of the most fundamental physical principles we know. A flag on the Moon is, for us on Earth, an object that physics itself has declared off‑limits — not because we aren’t clever enough yet, but because the universe has a strict pixel limit set by the wavelength of light and photon statistics. It’s like the speed of light: no amount of engineering will let us exceed it. Similarly, no amount of telescope progress will let us resolve something that is both **angularly tiny** and **buried in an overwhelming background glare**. The flag is a double‑whammy that puts it forever beyond the reach of any Earth‑based telescope, no matter how advanced.
