ct radar target. Also, active illumination can't overcome the fundamental problem of angular resolution: a radar or lidar system still has a resolution limit set by its aperture (antenna size) relative to wavelength. To resolve the flag at optical wavelengths with lidar, you'd need a huge telescope anyway. At radio wavelengths, you'd need an even larger antenna. So active techniques don't bypass the resolution limit. And even if they did, the contrast problem remains: the flag doesn't have a unique signature that separates it from the surrounding terrain. We might detect something if the flag were made of metal and acted as a corner reflector, but it's not. The Apollo flags were nylon and aluminum foil (the pole? Actually the pole was aluminum, but it's thin and not a corner reflector). Even the pole would be hard to distinguish from natural rocks. We need to answer in a friendly, respectful tone, acknowledging the user's clever idea, and then explaining why it wouldn't work for this specific case, while perhaps mentioning that we can detect the lunar landers using lunar orbiters, but not from Earth. This might be a good time to summarize the two key barriers: resolution and contrast, and note that active illumination doesn't solve either. Maybe we can reference the fact that we already use lasers to range to the retroreflectors, which proves active illumination works, but the flag is a poor target. The user's thinking is commendable; this is exactly how scientists overcome obstacles. But here, the obstacles are too fundamental. I'll structure the response: 1) Acknowledge the creativity and the Venus analogy. 2) Explain how radar and laser illumination work, and why they succeed for Venus mapping and retroreflectors, respectively. 3) Point out the differences: Venus mapping uses large ground-based radar dishes (Arecibo) to achieve high resolution via synthetic aperture processing, targeting topography; the flag is tiny and featureless as a radar target. For laser, b